.0522, 0.043, 0.0657], "Social": [0.1399, 0.1281, 0.1561]}}, "facts": "DATASET: UCI Online Shoppers Purchasing Intention (Sakar & Kastro, 2018): one year of sessions on an online store. 12,205 sessions after removing duplicates; overall conversion rate 15.6%.\nVISITOR TYPE: new visitors convert at 24.9%, returning visitors at 14.1% (chi-square p=4.2e-29). Returning visitors are 85% of sessions.\nMONTH: Feb 1.7% (181 sessions), Mar 10.3% (1860 sessions), May 11.0% (3329 sessions), June 10.2% (285 sessions), Jul 15.3% (432 sessions), Aug 17.6% (433 sessions), Sep 19.2% (448 sessions), Oct 20.9% (549 sessions), Nov 25.5% (2982 sessions), Dec 12.7% (1706 sessions). Best Nov, worst Feb (chi-square p=1.6e-75). Jan and Apr are missing from the data.\nTRAFFIC SOURCE (anonymised numeric codes; only codes with 100+ sessions): type 8: 27.7% of 343, type 20: 25.9% of 193, type 2: 21.7% of 3911, type 5: 21.5% of 260, type 10: 20.0% of 450, type 11: 19.0% of 247, type 4: 15.5% of 1066, type 6: 12.0% of 443, type 1: 11.0% of 2388, type 3: 8.9% of 2013, type 13: 5.9% of 728.\nWEEKEND: weekend sessions convert at 17.5% vs weekdays 15.1%.\nCONVERTED vs NOT (medians, all differences significant by Mann-Whitney U, p<0.05): product pages 29 vs 16; time on product pages 18.5 vs 8.8 minutes; mean bounce rate 0.005 vs 0.023; mean exit rate 0.020 vs 0.046.\nPREDICTIVE MODELS (held-out ROC-AUC): with PageValues, logistic regression 0.906, random forest 0.932. Without PageValues, logistic 0.742, random forest 0.775. PageValues partly leaks the outcome (it is computed from pages seen before a transaction), so the 'without' model is the honest view of early-session signals.\nTOP DRIVERS WITHOUT PAGEVALUES (random-forest permutation importance): Time on product pages (s), Exit rate, Month: Nov, Product pages viewed, Account/admin pages viewed.\nACTION (suggestions from the research): re-engage returning visitors, who are most of the traffic but convert least, for example with remarketing or personalised offers; concentrate campaign budget in the run-up to November; send paid traffic straight to product pages and work on reducing exits there, since time on product pages is the strongest early signal of a purchase.\nCAVEATS: associations, not causation. Traffic sources are numeric codes with no channel names. This dataset is session-level, not multi-touch; channel attribution results will come from the Google Analytics 360 sample and Criteo datasets (in progress).\nCRITEO DISPLAY ADS: 16,468,027 impressions, 6,142,256 users, 675 campaigns, 435,810 conversions over 30 days. 99.3% of conversions involve a single campaign, so the data cannot compare channels or campaigns within a journey. Converting journeys average 1.85 clicks; 68% have one click.\nCRITEO LAST-CLICK GAP: only 54.2% of conversions that followed a Criteo click were credited to Criteo under the advertiser's last-click rule; the rest went to other channels. Credit depends on recency: 87% when the conversion came within an hour of the click, 36% after 1-3 days and 26% after 7-31 days, while the number of clicks barely changes it.\nVALIDATION SIMULATION (known true channel effects, 6 random worlds each): when ads act immediately, mean absolute error in credit share was First touch 10.5 pp (rank corr 0.17); Last touch 4.2 pp (rank corr 0.66); Linear 5.2 pp (rank corr 0.68); Time decay 4.7 pp (rank corr 0.66); Position based 5.8 pp (rank corr 0.67); Markov chain 6.4 pp (rank corr 0.61); Shapley value 5.5 pp (rank corr 0.69). When ads act with carry-over (awareness first), it was First touch 10.1 pp (rank corr 0.18); Last touch 7.3 pp (rank corr 0.37); Linear 5.7 pp (rank corr 0.60); Time decay 5.7 pp (rank corr 0.50); Position based 6.7 pp (rank corr 0.56); Markov chain 7.5 pp (rank corr 0.45); Shapley value 6.1 pp (rank corr 0.60). Best: Last touch (immediate), Time decay (carry-over); first touch was worst in both, so no single attribution model is best everywhere.\nGA360 CHANNEL JOURNEYS (Google Merchandise Store, 12 months): 714,167 visitors, 903,653 sessions, 10,022 converting journeys (1.4% of journeys) followed across sessions up to the first purchase. 52% of converting journeys took more than one session (mean 2.28) but only 12% used more than one channel. Most common multi-channel paths: Direct > Referral (134); Organic Search > Referral (94); Direct > Referral > Referral (54); Paid Search > Organic Search (39).\nGA360 ATTRIBUTION, all converting journeys: models broadly agree. Last touch: Referral 45%, Organic Search 31%, Direct 17%, Paid Search 4%, Display 1%. Markov: Referral 43%, Organic Search 32%, Direct 18%, Paid Search 4%, Display 1%, Social 1%.\nGA360 ATTRIBUTION, the 1,189 multi-channel journeys: first touch: Direct 42%, Organic Search 30%, Referral 13%, Paid Search 9%, Display 4%, Social 1%; last touch: Referral 60%, Organic Search 26%, Paid Search 7%, Display 3%, Social 2%, Direct 1%; linear: Referral 38%, Organic Search 28%, Direct 19%, Paid Search 8%, Display 5%, Social 2%. Direct starts journeys, Referral ends them.\nGA360 CAVEATS: Google Analytics labels a returning direct visit with the previous campaign source (40,356 of 104,838 Referral sessions are flagged as direct), so Direct rarely appears late in a journey; 97% of converting Referral sessions record no referring site.", "ga360": {"period": "20160801 to 20170801", "sessions": 903653, "visitors": 714167, "converting_sessions": 11552, "conversions": 10022, "nonconverting_journeys": 704145, "conv_rate": 0.014033132306589355, "multi_session_share": 0.5214528038315706, "multi_channel_share": 0.118638994212732, "sessions_mean": 2.2793853522251046, "sessions_max": 138, "days_first_to_purchase_median_multi": 4.7067245370370365, "nonconv_sessions_mean": 1.2319280829942696, "channels": [{"channel": "Organic Search", "sessions": 381561, "converting": 3443, "rate": 0.009023458896480511}, {"channel": "Social", "sessions": 226117, "converting": 104, "rate": 0.0004599388811986715}, {"channel": "